<Post title="Lobotomising the vendor apps" part={3} date="2026-08-16">
	<p class="tagline">
		The vendor's two apps talk to each other over Android broadcasts guarded by a
		signature-level permission. My replacement service can't hold the vendor's signing key —
		so the apps got a small, precise piece of surgery.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>how the vendor apps talk</h2>
	<p>
		MyAir's Android stack is two apps: <code>myair5</code> (the MyPlace UI) and <code
			>aaservice</code
		> (the background service that owns the USB link to the control
		box). They communicate via system broadcasts — and reading the decompiled source, every
		message type exists in <strong>two parallel variants</strong>:
	</p>

	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>channel</th><th>guard</th><th>payload</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><code>MESSAGE_FROM_CB_SECURE</code>, <code>CAN_TO_CB</code>, <code>BROADCAST_CAN_TO_CB</code>, <code>BACKUP_MESSAGE</code>…</td>
				<td><code>com.air.android.secure_comms</code> — <code>protectionLevel="signature"</code></td>
				<td>plaintext strings</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>…_NO_PERMISSION</code> variants</td>
				<td>none</td>
				<td>AES-encrypted <code>byte[]</code></td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>
		The "encrypted" channel is security theatre, incidentally: both apps embed the same
		hardcoded AES key, recoverable by anyone who decompiles the publicly distributed APK.
	</p>
	<pre><code>Key:   +07UDwu4yLmTkTpOYxe9Vc4K/2slMFRWrcvN2tuFxvc=   (Base64)
Alg:   AES/CBC/PKCS7Padding, IV = 16 zero bytes
Quirk: 3 random bytes are prepended to the plaintext before
       encryption (strip them after decrypting)</code></pre>

	<Mermaid>{`sequenceDiagram
		participant M as myair5 (MyPlace UI)
		participant A as aaservice
		participant C as Control Box
		M->>A: GET_DATA / GET_ALL_DATA (no guard)
		A->>C: USB / RS-485 register poll
		C-->>A: register data
		A->>M: MESSAGE_FROM_CB (unguarded, plaintext)
		A->>M: MESSAGE_FROM_CB_SECURE (signature-guarded)
		Note over M,A: the guarded lane only flows if both apps<br/>share a signing key`}</Mermaid>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the wall</h2>
	<p>
		My rebuilt service (<code>aaservice2</code>, package renamed, signed with my own key)
		can't send or receive the signature-guarded broadcasts: the framework checks the
		caller's signing certificate against the certificate of the app that declared the
		permission. Root alone doesn't bypass this — the check lives in <code
			>PackageManagerService</code>, not in either app.
	</p>
	<p>Options considered:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>LSPosed hook</strong> on <code>PackageManagerService.checkPermission()</code> to return GRANTED for <code>secure_comms</code> — elegant, survives app updates, but adds a
			framework-hooking dependency to a wall-mounted appliance
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Use only the <code>_NO_PERMISSION</code> lanes</strong> — covers most of the
			protocol, but not every receiver path
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Patch the apps' manifests</strong> so the permission is no longer
			signature-guarded at all — crude name, clean result: <em>the lobotomy</em>
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the lobotomy</h2>
	<p>
		The entire modification is two lines across two manifests:
		<code>protectionLevel="signature"</code> → <code>"normal"</code> for <code
			>com.air.android.secure_comms</code
		> (and MyPlace's <code>DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION</code>). Both apps are
		then rebuilt and re-signed with a throwaway key — and since both are now signed by the <em>same</em> throwaway key, even the remaining signature semantics are satisfied. Any
		app on the device can now join the broadcast protocol.
	</p>
	<p>The full, reproducible recipe:</p>

	<pre><code># 1. Decompile
apktool d MyPlace_15.1514.apk -o myplace/decompiled
apktool d app-aaservice2-v14.150.apk -o aaservice/decompiled

# 2. Relax the permission guard in both manifests
sed -i 's/protectionLevel="signature"/protectionLevel="normal"/g' \
  myplace/decompiled/AndroidManifest.xml \
  aaservice/decompiled/AndroidManifest.xml

# 3. Only ONE installed app may declare a permission — keep the
#    &lt;permission&gt; block in aaservice, delete it from MyPlace's manifest
#    (MyPlace keeps its &lt;uses-permission&gt;)

# 4. MyPlace ships native libs page-aligned &amp; uncompressed; apktool's
#    rebuild breaks that, so let the package manager extract them instead
sed -i 's/extractNativeLibs="false"/extractNativeLibs="true"/' \
  myplace/decompiled/AndroidManifest.xml

# 5. Rebuild
apktool b myplace/decompiled -o MyPlace-lobotomized-unsigned.apk
apktool b aaservice/decompiled -o aaservice-lobotomized-unsigned.apk

# 6. Zipalign (mandatory for the MyPlace rebuild)
zipalign -f 4 MyPlace-lobotomized-unsigned.apk MyPlace-aligned.apk

# 7. Sign with a throwaway key
keytool -genkey -keystore lobotomy.jks -alias lobotomy \
  -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000
apksigner sign --ks lobotomy.jks --ks-key-alias lobotomy \
  --out MyPlace-lobotomized.apk MyPlace-aligned.apk
apksigner sign --ks lobotomy.jks --ks-key-alias lobotomy \
  --out aaservice-lobotomized.apk aaservice-lobotomized-unsigned.apk</code></pre>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>things that went wrong (so you don't have to)</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>error</th><th>cause</th><th>fix</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_INVALID_APK: Failed to extract native libraries</code></td>
				<td>MyPlace sets <code>extractNativeLibs="false"</code> — its <code>.so</code> files are stored uncompressed and page-aligned in the APK, and apktool's rebuild loses the alignment</td>
				<td>Flip <code>extractNativeLibs</code> to <code>true</code> (step 4) + zipalign</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_DUPLICATE_PERMISSION</code></td>
				<td>Both apps declare <code>secure_comms</code>; Android allows exactly one declarer</td>
				<td>Delete the <code>&lt;permission&gt;</code> block from MyPlace, keep it in aaservice</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE</code></td>
				<td>Re-signed APK over a vendor-signed install</td>
				<td>Uninstall first (back up app data beforehand — NeoBackup)</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><code>DELETE_FAILED_DEVICE_POLICY_MANAGER</code></td>
				<td>aaservice holds a device-admin receiver</td>
				<td><code>dpm remove-active-admin com.air.advantage.aaservice2/.receiver.DeviceAdminReceiver</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>App won't start after in-place APK swap</td>
				<td>Stale <code>oat/</code> compiled from the original APK</td>
				<td>Delete the app's <code>oat</code> dir + <code>code_cache</code>, restart</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>"Display over other apps" greyed out</td>
				<td>Restricted in settings on this ROM</td>
				<td><code>appops set com.air.advantage.myair5 SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW allow</code></td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>verifying the bypass</h2>
	<p>
		Post-install, fire each of the previously-forbidden actions from a shell (which holds no
		signature permission at all) and confirm the framework lets them through:
	</p>
	<pre><code>am broadcast -a com.air.advantage.MESSAGE_FROM_CB_SECURE \
  --es "com.air.advantage.GET_DATA_REQUEST" "verification" \
  --es "com.air.advantage.MESSAGE_FROM_CB_SECURE" "signature_bypass_confirmed"
# → result=0 for all four guarded actions; dumpsys shows
#   com.air.android.secure_comms: granted=true</code></pre>

	<div class="callout warn">
		<span class="callout-label">security note</span>
		This removes the signature barrier entirely — any app on the device can now inject
		control messages. Acceptable on a dedicated, VLAN-isolated control tablet; do not do
		this on a device you install other things on.
	</div>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>from patched to rewritten</h2>
	<p>
		The lobotomised pair was the bridge: it kept the system fully working while I built the
		real replacement. Patching smali gets you a working app once; it doesn't get you a
		maintainable one. The endgame is <a href="https://github.com/retsimx/aaservice">retsimx/aaservice</a> — a clean-room
		had: a <strong>dual transport</strong>. It can drive the control box over the original
		USB accessory path, <em>or</em> as a WebSocket client to a daemon running somewhere else
		entirely.
	</p>
	<p>That daemon is part 4.</p>
</Post>
